################ python/mypy#18176
Add support for TypedDict extra_items= and closed= (PEP 728) | OPEN  | created 2024-11-21 closed - | labels: feature,topic-typed-dict,priority-0-high | author mharding-hpe
--- body
Edit from maintainers: mypy supports closed TypedDicts since version 2.2

---

**Feature**

[PEP 728](https://peps.python.org/pep-0728/) proposes changes to `TypedDict`, including the ability to specify the types of unspecified keys.

This has been [implemented in `typing_extensions` with the `__extra_items__` keyword](https://typing-extensions.readthedocs.io/en/latest/index.html#typing_extensions.TypedDict.__extra_items__) since version 4.10.

I would like to see support for this added to `mypy`.

**Pitch**

I am working on using mypy to validate Python code associated with rest API services. It is not uncommon for the OpenAPI specs we use to include objects whose `additionalProperties` values map to schemas. This is how OpenAPI handles the case of objects whose keywords are not known in advance, but whose values must be of a particular type.

I want to be able to type annotate these schemas in Python, but currently there is no good mechanism for doing this. That is one of the problems that PEP 728 attempts to solve.

Currently with mypy I have found a few ways to handle this (in the absence of adding the requested support):

1. As noted in the PEP, one can always suppress the `typeddict-unknown-key` errors. But of course that means no type checking is happening for the additional keys being used.
2. I haven't coded it up yet, but I suspect with overloading I could probably come up with something that works for this. Basically, I could overload the getitem/setitem methods in a Protocol, specifying the correct return types for the various pre-defined keys (as Literals), and then add "generic" fallback get/set item methods for the "extra key type".
3. It's possible I could come up with some Frankensteining of TypedDicts and defaultdicts that accomplishes this. Although t
--- comments (5)
[layday 2024-11-22] The PEP has been updated and no longer specifies the `__extra_items__` *property*.  `typing_extensions` does not yet support the new parametrised form.
~~
[mharding-hpe 2024-11-22] Good catch -- I missed that. In that case, I think it makes sense to wait for `typing_extensions` to catch up with the PEP before supporting it in mypy. 

I have opened [a `typing_extensions` issue for this](https://github.com/python/typing_extensions/issues/511), as I do not see one currently open.
~~
[mharding-hpe 2025-03-21] The `typing_extensions` work just got completed for this 
~~
[JukkaL 2025-06-05] I think all the prerequisites are ready to start work on the implementation -- we just need somebody who has time and interest to work on this.
~~
[peacefulotter 2026-01-07] Any news on this? Thanks ! 
################ python/mypy#20591
Improve error messages when positional argument is missing | MERGED  | created 2026-01-16 closed 2026-03-30T16:57:24Z | labels:  | author KevinRK29
--- body
## Summary

This PR improves error messages when positional argument is missing from a function call. 

Previously when a user forgets a positional argument, mypy would previously emit multiple type errors because the subsequent arguments would be "shifted" and mismatched with their expected types. Instead of showing multiple type errors, it emits a single consolidated message that suggests which argument might be missing.
